<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Sambas Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1675 CE – 1950 CE (approx.) · capital Sambas</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Malay sultanate of north-western Borneo with Bruneian roots, whose lands drew Chinese gold miners and their self-governing kongsis.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sambas was founded in 1675 by Raden Sulaiman, a prince of Bruneian royal descent, who took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Muhammad Shafiuddin I. He displaced an earlier Javanese-linked ruling house on the Sambas river, and the sultanate grew into the main Malay power of the north-western coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The sultans ruled coastal Malay communities and drew tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayak-people">Dayak</a> villages inland. From the 18th century they invited Chinese miners to work the gold fields, but the miners formed powerful kongsis such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/heshun-confederation">Heshun Confederation</a> that escaped the court. Sambas was attacked by the British in 1813 for harbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> made Sambas a protected state in 1818 and crushed the kongsis in the 1850s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Muhammad Ibrahim Shafiuddin was killed by the Japanese in 1944. The sultanate was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Republic of Indonesia</a> in 1950; its palace survives in Sambas town.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sambas-sultanate.jpg" alt="Sambas Sultanate" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ibrahim Muizzuddin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Malay_Banner_(Yellow).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1675 CE – 1950 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sambas</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad Shafiuddin I, Muhammad Ibrahim Shafiuddin</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sambas Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Sambas</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1812&amp;focus=sambas-sultanate" aria-label="Sambas Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7716 2127 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sambas Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8036 2383L7959 2663M8036 2383L7959 2664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7959 2663h0M7959 2664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8036 2383h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8036 2383h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sambas. <a href="/atlas?year=1812&amp;focus=sambas-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
